  "query_preview": "..\nClear skies across southeast Arizona this morning as \nstrong high pressure aloft over western New Mexico is the dominant \nfeature of our weather pattern. Moisture levels are below normal \nacross the region, but most notably from Tucson eastward with PWAT \nvalues 0.7\" to 0.9\" at this time, with surface dewpoints mostly in \nthe 40s to lower 50s. A bit more moisture west of Tucson where PWATs \nrange from 1 to 1.2 inches. Storm activity will struggle once again \ntoday as the strong high nudging west to the AZ/NM border results in \nbroad subsidence (with dry air aloft). Can't rule out a storm or two \nmainly to the south to southwest of Tucson from Santa Cruz into \nsouth central Pima County where moisture and instability levels are \nmore supportive and the subsidence under the high isn't as \npronounced. The hottest temperatures of this extended heat stretch \nare expected today, with the current forecast of 109 degrees in \nTucson. Wouldn't be surprised if it ends up slightly higher (110 or \n111) if stronger mixing of drier air aloft occurs dropping surface \ndewpoints aiding surface heating.\n\nWhile there won't be much change with the mid/upper level high on \nThursday, centered near or just west of the AZ/NM border, lower \nlevel moisture will start to be on the increase. This will begin to \noccur late tonight/Thursday morning as a likely first modest push of \nmoisture occurs courtesy of outflow from convection in Sonora. This \nwill set the stage for a few more storms (mostly 10 to 20 percent \nchance) on Thursday with the focus still on locales south to \nsouthwest of Tucson. Wind will be the primary concern with any \nstorms on Thursday. \n\nFrom Friday through Monday, we will quickly transition into a much \nmore active monsoon pattern. During this time, the mid/upper level \nhigh will be on the move eastward into a more favorable position \nover New Mexico by Saturday, with the stage being set for broader \ndeep layer southeast to southerly flow. This will result in \nsustained above normal moisture with 110 to 150 percent of normal \nPWAT values. Troughing over the NW part of the state could also \nprovide a little bit of additional lift especially Sunday/Monday. \nBottom line is this will translate into storm chances in the 20 to \n50 percent range Friday increasing to 30 to 60 percent Saturday \nthrough Monday. As is usual for the monsoon, we will likely have a \nmore 'up' day or two but it's too far out to really get a good feel \nfor the most active days. As deeper moisture moves in, we'll also be \nadding increased flash flooding potential in addition to wind \nthreats. Thereafter, back to a more typical lower to mid to start \nthe month of September but still considerable uncertainty that far \nout. \n\nThe Extreme Heat Warning remains in effect through Saturday. \nAdmittedly, temperatures on Saturday are expected to drop a few \ndegrees compared to today through Friday. We'll continue to monitor \nthat for any later adjustments in the timing of the Extreme Heat \nWarning. By Sunday into early next week, temperatures drop to more \nseasonable levels.",
